{
  "concept": "A parabola's vertex sets both its turning point and its axis of symmetry, and the quadratic's stretch factor controls how far the x-intercepts sit from that axis.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Cartesian grid with light gray dotted gridlines at every integer",
    "Horizontal x-axis from -6 to 6 with integer tick labels and 'x' label at the right end, arrowheads on both ends",
    "Vertical y-axis from -6 to 6 with integer tick labels and 'y' label at the top, arrowheads on both ends",
    "Heavy teal upward-opening parabola f(x) = (x-1)^2 - 4, with arrowheads on both rising branches near y = 5",
    "Filled black dot at the vertex (1, -4)",
    "Filled black dots at the x-intercepts (-1, 0) and (3, 0)",
    "Filled black dot at the y-intercept (0, -3)",
    "Filled black dot at the mirror partner of the y-intercept, (2, -3)",
    "Red dashed vertical line through x = 1 spanning the visible y-range (axis of symmetry)",
    "Label 'Axis of symmetry' at top right with a black arrow pointing to the red dashed line",
    "Label 'x-intercepts' at right with two black arrows pointing to (-1,0) and (3,0)",
    "Label 'y-intercept' at lower left with an arrow pointing to (0,-3)",
    "Label 'Vertex' at bottom with an arrow pointing to (1,-4)",
    "Dynamic readout of the current equation in vertex form, y = a(x - h)^2 + k",
    "Optional horizontal dashed connector between the y-intercept and its mirror point showing equal heights",
    "Optional equal-distance braces from the axis of symmetry to each x-intercept"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "dragVertex",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "The vertex alone determines the turning point AND the location of the axis of symmetry: move it and the dashed line, the mirror pair, and the intercepts all follow."
    },
    {
      "id": "stretch",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Stretch factor a",
      "range": [0.25, 3, 0.25],
      "default": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "annotationView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Highlight",
      "options": ["All labels", "Intercepts", "Symmetry"],
      "default": "All labels"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The labeled parabola",
      "narration": "The teal curve y = (x-1)^2 - 4 turns at the vertex (1, -4), crosses the x-axis at -1 and 3, and crosses the y-axis at -3. Every feature has a name.",
      "state": { "annotationView": "All labels", "stretch": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reading the intercepts",
      "narration": "Focus on the crossings only: two x-intercepts where the curve meets y = 0, and one y-intercept where it meets x = 0. These are the solutions and the starting height of the quadratic.",
      "state": { "annotationView": "Intercepts", "stretch": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The mirror line",
      "narration": "Now emphasize symmetry. The red dashed line through the vertex reflects the curve onto itself: the y-intercept at (0,-3) has a partner at (2,-3), the same height and the same distance from x = 1.",
      "state": { "annotationView": "Symmetry", "stretch": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Drag the vertex",
      "narration": "Drag the vertex anywhere on the plane. The dashed axis of symmetry slides to stay under it, the mirror pair stays level, and the x-intercepts move — or disappear when the vertex rises above the x-axis.",
      "state": { "annotationView": "All labels", "stretch": 1 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Stretch and squeeze",
      "narration": "Increase a and the parabola narrows, pulling the x-intercepts toward the axis of symmetry; decrease a and they spread apart. The axis itself never moves.",
      "state": { "annotationView": "Intercepts", "stretch": 2.5 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The vertex is the anchor of the whole graph — it fixes the minimum and the mirror line — while the coefficient a only controls how fast the curve widens, so it slides the x-intercepts toward or away from that fixed line.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Square coordinate plane, both axes -6 to 6, light gray dotted grid at integers, thin black axes with arrowheads and 'x' / 'y' labels at the right and top. Plot a heavy teal (steel-blue) upward parabola in vertex form with arrowheads on both branches, clipped near y = 5-6. Mark five small filled black dots: vertex (1,-4), x-intercepts (-1,0) and (3,0), y-intercept (0,-3), and mirror point (2,-3). Draw the axis of symmetry as a bold red dashed vertical line through the vertex. Black label text with thin curved arrows: 'Axis of symmetry' upper right pointing down to the red line, 'x-intercepts' mid right with two arrows to the two zeros, 'y-intercept' on the left pointing right to (0,-3), 'Vertex' at bottom pointing up to the turning point. When the 'Intercepts' view is active, dim the symmetry line and pulse the three intercept dots; when 'Symmetry' is active, dim the intercept labels and emphasize the red line plus a light dashed horizontal connector between (0,-3) and (2,-3). Show a live vertex-form equation readout in a corner that updates with drag and slider."
}

