        "stretch": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Stretch and squeeze",
      "narration": "Increase a and the parabola narrows, pulling the x-intercepts toward the axis of symmetry; decrease a and they spread apart. The axis itself never moves.",
      "state": {
        "annotationView": "Intercepts",
        "stretch": 2.5
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The vertex is the anchor of the whole graph — it fixes the minimum and the mirror line — while the coefficient a only controls how fast the curve widens, so it slides the x-intercepts toward or away from that fixed line.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Square coordinate plane, both axes -6 to 6, light gray dotted grid at integers, thin black axes with arrowheads and 'x' / 'y' labels at the right and top. Plot a heavy teal (steel-blue) upward parabola in vertex form with arrowheads on both branches, clipped near y = 5-6. Mark five small filled black dots: vertex (1,-4), x-intercepts (-1,0) and (3,0), y-intercept (0,-3), and mirror point (2,-3). Draw the axis of symmetry as a bold red dashed vertical line through the vertex. Black label text with thin curved arrows: 'Axis of symmetry' upper right pointing down to the red line, 'x-intercepts' mid right with two arrows to the two zeros, 'y-intercept' on the left pointing right to (0,-3), 'Vertex' at bottom pointing up to the turning point. When the 'Intercepts' view is active, dim the symmetry line and pulse the three intercept dots; when 'Symmetry' is active, dim the intercept labels and emphasize the red line plus a light dashed horizontal connector between (0,-3) and (2,-3). Show a live vertex-form equation readout in a corner that updates with drag and slider."
}

